# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv*/
venv*/
.env
.pytest_cache/
*.pytest_cache

# Data & model caches (gitignored — pulled at runtime)
data/
*.cache
.cache/
models/

# Training/output
out/
training/out/
eval/outputs/
*.report.json

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# Playwright MCP probe artifacts
.playwright-mcp/
_private/

# Audit artifacts
audit_report*.md
delegation-*

# Root-scratch policy (issues #38, #42). One-off diagnostic scratch at the repo root
# is throwaway by definition and ignored, per the committed-probes convention above.
# Anchored to the root so similar-looking `.claude/probes/` files (e.g.
# `.claude/probes/_fuzz_*.jsonl`, `.claude/probes/sliceN_*.py`) stay tracked.
/_mem_probe*/
/audit_tmp.json
/_fix_*.py
/_probe*.py
/slice[0-9]*_*

# Captured command output. Fourteen of these were committed by accident: a redirect to
# an absolute Windows scratchpad path was taken literally, so `C:\...\foo_err.log` became
# a single file in the repo root, and `git add -A` swept it in across five commits. No
# log belongs in version control, so ignore the extension rather than the one spelling.
*.log

# Agent scratch. `.claude/worktrees/`, `.claude/tasks/` and `.claude/records/` are
# per-run working state, ignored by name (not by directory) so that adding something
# shareable under `.claude/` later is a deliberate act, not a silent one. These
# entries lived in .git/info/exclude, which is per-clone: it protected exactly one
# machine, and a detached worktree would have shown up as untracked for everyone else.
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/tasks/
# Per-worker rows, collected into the log by the fleet runner and then deleted.
.claude/records/

# Committed-probes convention (issues #38, #42). Probe scripts AND their outputs live
# in `.claude/probes/` and are committed — `.claude/probes/sliceN_*.py` plus their
# .jsonl/.txt outputs (314 tracked files at wave 4). `.claude/probes/` is therefore
# deliberately NOT ignored wholesale; the only ignores that apply there are generic
# ones like `*.log` / `__pycache__/`. Scratch that is not meant to be kept lives
# OUTSIDE the repo (e.g. the OS temp dir); anything scratch-named that does appear at
# the repo root matches a root-scratch pattern below and stays untracked-and-ignored.

# mkdocs build output. `mkdocs build --strict` is part of the gate suite, so this
# directory appears after every verification run; `git add -A` would commit the whole
# rendered site. It has happened once already, with 14 stray log files.
site/
